  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Express $[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ]^{11}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ]^{11}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "De Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\phi )]^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=11$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ]^{11}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(11\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(11\\theta ).\n$$",
